xygen  binding into myoglobin in form of an  equation so here mbo2  is a oxygen bound myoglobin which can be  dissociated to myoglobin and oxygen  let us try to understand k d is the  dissociation constant so k  d is given by concentration of myoglobin  multiplied by oxygen concentration  divided by  bound myoglobin concentration now we can  derive one term which is known as  fractional saturation  so fractional saturation of y is equal  to number of occupied binding sites  divided by total number of binding sites  which would be given by this expression  and now if we explain it in terms of  partial pressure  fractional saturation would come like  this  partial pressure of oxygen divided by  po2 plus  the dissociation constant and if we plot  this  it would take a sigmoid shape just like  a michaelis-menten equation  it's pretty much similar right now let's  talk about  the oxygen binding kinetics in the  hemoglobin  now here we have a protein which is our  hemoglobin  and the ligand is oxygen now  n number of ligand can bind to this  protein and form  this particular protein ligand complex  so in case of hemoglobin this n  equal to 4 now let us try to  give association constant here k  association and the reverse would be  dissociation constant  so we can clearly write the association  constant  as this kind of expression  now we can also derive the  binding fraction or saturation fraction  which would be  binding sites which are occupied divided  by  total number of binding sites and if we  do our  math we would derive an equation like  this  and by replacing it with the partial  pressure of oxygen  we would ultimate ultimately generate  another equation  so this particular equation  if we plot it in the graph it would  kind of come like a  so this particular equation if we take  log on the both side  and plot it in a graph it would show a  sigmoidal curve  so this curve is pretty much different  from michaelis-menten equation now if we  have  a value of n which 